# round4_q3: proof of the nerve statement (#19) for every monoid P

**Plan.** Prove #19 as stated, for every P, by reducing it to the fine case:
(a) a compactness argument (first-order compactness theorem) gives a finite subcover;
(b) descent of f.p. objects to a fine level P_{S'};
(c) a second compactness argument makes the descended finite family cover all valuations at a suitable fine level;
(d) the kernel conditions defining N_tau are detected at a finite level;
(e) the closed-convex nerve argument of #15, Steps 1-4, is applied at that fine level.

**Result.** #19 is TRUE for every P. The proof never uses birhizomicity. It uses only the weaker property that every valuation (with divisible value group) of Q factors through some Q_i. It also does not need tau to be principal: any face tau with Phi_Q ⊂ tau works.

(Status: in progress. The final status section is at the end.)

summary:
I proved statement #19 as stated, for every monoid P. Combined with the existing chain, that answers the headline question YES, assuming the facts I cited from memory.

**The proof of #19 has three steps.**
1. **A finite subfamily is enough.** I put the space of all valuations of Q (as total preorders on Q^gp ⊗ Q) in the product topology, where it is compact. Each member Q_i is a retract of a finite presentation. So by Fourier–Motzkin, "w factors through Q_i" is equivalent to w(y_s) ≥ 0 for finitely many fixed elements y_s, the same test for every divisible ordered value group. These loci are clopen and cover the space, so finitely many members J_0 suffice.
2. **Mixing witnesses inside a real closed field K.** Take a finite subcomplex L, put J' = vert(L) ∪ J_0, and choose an injective "witness" valuation for each simplex of N_τ(J'). I embed all of these into one real closed field K and mix them as w_λ = Σ λ_m v_m with λ in the K-simplex. Each w_λ has kernel on P inside τ. The set of λ for which w_λ factors through Q_i is a closed convex K-polytope, these polytopes cover the simplex, and their nerve is exactly N_τ(J').
3. **Transfer.** The nerve theorem for finite closed convex covers of a simplex holds over R. For fixed sizes it is a first-order statement, so by Tarski it holds over K. Hence N_τ(J') has vanishing reduced homology. Every finite subcomplex lies in such an N_τ(J'), so N_τ is acyclic, and so are its cochain complexes with any coefficients.

Two consequences you may not expect. Only the weak cover property is used ("every valuation factors through some member"); birhizomicity itself and any openness lemma are unnecessary. Taking λ in K removes the half-open-convex-set problem, and compactness replaces Lemma K, which also gives a shorter proof of the finitely generated case.

I re-checked the director's valuation description of N_τ and the chain #15/#16/#19 (steps 1–6) and found no error.

**Not proved here; cited from memory:**
- compact objects are retracts of finite presentations;
- the Levi/Fuchs order-extension theorem;
- Farkas/Fourier–Motzkin and completeness of the theory of divisible ordered groups;
- embedding ordered groups into a real closed field (finite-rank Hahn plus compactness);
- the Borsuk/Leray nerve theorem;
- Tarski's completeness of real closed fields;
- Cartan's criterion (Stacks 03F7).

Nothing else is left open.

